---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Flow from '../../components/Flow.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Transaction risk — Christopher Uzochukwu"
  description="A synthetic card-not-present risk baseline: logistic regression, a frozen threshold, a population-stability check, and an offline shadow threshold."
>
  <h1>Transaction risk</h1>
  <p class="dateline">Christopher Uzochukwu · 27 October 2026 · Africa/Lagos</p>
  <p>
    Public repository: <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/transaction-risk">github.com/chrisuzor/transaction-risk</a>.
    Every row is synthetic. The generator draws each field from a fixed-seed random distribution.
  </p>

  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    Card-not-present payments are authorized without the physical card, so a risk score is often
    computed before a decision. This repository is a small public baseline of that shape: a
    fixed-seed generator, a logistic regression, one frozen threshold, and an HTTP API that returns
    a     score plus the threshold decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/transaction-risk/actions/workflows/ci.yml">
      <img
        class="badge"
        alt="Transaction risk CI status"
        src="https://github.com/chrisuzor/transaction-risk/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg"
        width="100"
        height="20"
      />
    </a>
  </p>
  <p>
    Architecture: <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/transaction-risk/blob/main/docs/model-card.md">docs/model-card.md</a>.
  </p>
  <Flow
    title="Transaction risk"
    description="Features go to a logistic regression, then a threshold, then the API. Drift is an offline check on the scores."
    caption="The API returns a score and the threshold decision. Drift compares score distributions. It is not a second request in the API path."
    steps={[
      { label: 'Features' },
      { label: 'Logistic regression' },
      { label: 'Threshold' },
      { label: 'API' },
      { label: 'Drift', note: 'offline check' },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Classical baseline</h2>
  <p>
    The baseline is a logistic regression, <code>baseline-v1</code>, trained with seed 42 on 20000
    synthetic rows. The split is stratified: 70% train, 15% validation, and 15% test (14000 / 3000
    / 3000). Labels are a noisy linear function of the derived features. The trainer does not
    read those label weights. Validation is used only to freeze the threshold. Precision, recall,
    and the confusion counts below are the test split. The API scores with the committed JSON
    coefficients.
  </p>

  <h2>Threshold</h2>
  <p>
    The operating point is recall-oriented. On the validation split the trainer keeps the highest
    threshold on a 0.001 grid with validation recall at least 0.90. That cutoff is 0.112.
    Validation recall at this threshold is 0.9045.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the held-out test split, at threshold 0.112, precision is 0.3558 and recall is 0.8803. False
    positives: 985. False negatives: 74. True positives: 544. True negatives: 1397. The synthetic
    positive rate on the test split is 0.2060. A lower threshold would flag more rows. A higher
    threshold would miss more synthetic fraud labels. No monetary loss is used.
  </p>

  <h2>Drift</h2>
  <p>
    Both windows are synthetic. The reference is the training split of the seed-42 generator. The
    unshifted holdout is the test split of that same draw. The shifted window is a separate
    fixed-seed draw of 3000 rows whose amounts, velocity, distance, ages, and flags come from
    different distributions. The statistic is the population stability index. A window is stable
    when every model-feature PSI and the score PSI are below 0.25. A value at or above 0.25 is not
    stable. The 0.25 cutoff is the usual significant-shift mark for PSI.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unshifted score PSI is 0.0030, and that window is stable. Shifted score PSI is 12.4338, and
    that window is not stable.
  </p>

  <h2>Shadow challenger</h2>
  <p>
    The champion is <code>baseline-v1</code>. Its threshold decision is the one the API would
    return. The challenger is a second threshold, 0.50, on those same scores. It is the conventional
    probability cutoff, stricter than the champion’s 0.112, and it is shadow-only. The batch is the
    synthetic test split: 3000 rows from seed 42.
  </p>
  <p>
    Agreement is 1826/3000. The challenger has 118 false positives and 381 false negatives.
    Every disagreement on this batch is a champion flag with a challenger allow. This comparison is
    an offline shadow on one synthetic batch.
  </p>

  <h2>Why a second algorithm was not used</h2>
  <p>
    A gradient boosting model trained on the same seed and the same split was a worse fit. The
    synthetic labels are a noisy linear function of the model features, so logistic regression is
    the matched model. On the test split the boosting fit had higher log loss (0.389 versus 0.374),
    a higher Brier score (0.122 versus 0.118), and more false positives at the same
    validation-recall rule (1067 versus 985). Gradient boosting is not a second algorithm in this
    repository. The challenger stays a 0.50 threshold on the logistic scores.
  </p>

  <h2>What it simulates</h2>
  <p>
    Every row is synthetic. The generator draws each field from a fixed-seed random distribution.
    The model is a logistic regression. The threshold, the population-stability check, and the
    shadow comparison at 0.50 all run on those rows. The HTTP API serves the frozen coefficients.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does not do</h2>
  <p>
    It does not use a real cardholder, a real card number, or a cloud account. It does not measure
    live traffic. No monetary loss is used to choose the threshold. Gradient boosting is not a
    second algorithm in this repository.
  </p>

  <h2>Tests</h2>
  <p>From the repository, after installing <code>requirements.txt</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>pytest</code></pre>
  <p>The latest CI run on main completed successfully. The workflow reruns training into a temporary directory, then pytest loads the committed model.</p>

  <h2>Example request</h2>
  <p>The API listens on port 8000. This body is row 0 of the default generator, saved as <code>models/known_example.json</code>.</p>
  <pre><code>uvicorn transaction_risk.api:app --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8000</code></pre>
  <pre><code>curl -sS -X POST http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/score \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -d '&#123;"amount":46.0,"hour_of_day":8,"day_of_week":6,"merchant_category":5,"international":false,"billing_shipping_mismatch":true,"txn_count_1h":1,"txn_count_24h":1,"mean_amount_7d":16.52,"device_age_days":145,"account_age_days":269,"km_from_prev":49.65&#125;'</code></pre>
  <p>
    The response includes <code>score</code>, <code>threshold</code>, <code>decision</code>
    (<code>flag</code> when the score is at least the threshold, otherwise <code>allow</code>),
    and <code>model_version</code>.
  </p>

  <p><a href="/contact/">Contact me</a></p>
</Base>
